          },
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "plt.figure(figsize=(12, 8))\n",
        "sns.heatmap(work.corr(), cmap=\"coolwarm\", center=0, annot=False)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Correlation Heatmap of Customer Features\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "0a167d6f",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0a167d6f"
      },
      "source": [
        "### 5.3 Scale the features\n",
        "\n",
        "Standard scaling gives each feature a mean near 0 and a standard deviation near 1. This is important because distance-based algorithms can otherwise be dominated by features with large numerical values."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 8,
      "id": "038cedaa",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "038cedaa",
        "outputId": "073b5f19-69ef-41c1-a855-aebb90f41df3"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Scaled data shape: (3999, 11)\n",
            "Mean of first five scaled features: [-0.  0.  0.  0.  0.]\n",
            "Standard deviation of first five scaled features: [1. 1. 1. 1. 1.]\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "scaler = StandardScaler()\n",
        "X_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(work)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Scaled data shape:\", X_scaled.shape)\n",
        "print(\"Mean of first five scaled features:\", np.round(X_scaled.mean(axis=0)[:5], 3))\n",
        "print(\"Standard deviation of first five scaled features:\", np.round(X_scaled.std(axis=0)[:5], 3))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "e2accd9e",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "e2accd9e"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 6. K-Means clustering\n",
        "\n",
        "K-Means assigns each customer to one of K groups. It repeatedly moves each cluster centre (centroid) to reduce the distance between customers and their assigned centre.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6.1 Elbow method\n",
        "\n",
        "We fit K-Means for several values of K and plot the inertia (within-cluster sum of squares). The “elbow” is where improvement begins to slow down. It is a guide, not an absolute rule."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 9,
      "id": "6718d0f8",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 501
        },
        "id": "6718d0f8",
        "outputId": "33a95d9a-895a-4529-df02-7b0b3b4de459"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "display_data",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "<Figure size 800x500 with 1 Axes>"
            ],
            "image/png": "\n"
          },
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "inertias = []\n",
        "k_values = range(2, 11)\n",
        "\n",
        "for k in k_values:\n",
        "    model = KMeans(n_clusters=k, random_state=RANDOM_STATE, n_init=10)\n",
        "    model.fit(X_scaled)\n",
        "    inertias.append(model.inertia_)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
        "plt.plot(list(k_values), inertias, marker=\"o\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Number of clusters (K)\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Inertia\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Elbow Method for K-Means\")\n",
        "plt.xticks(list(k_values))\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "98afe84c",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "98afe84c"
      },
      "source": [
