      "source": [
        "## 7. Hierarchical (Agglomerative) Clustering\n",
        "\n",
        "Hierarchical clustering starts with individual customers and repeatedly joins the closest groups. The `linkage` setting controls how the distance between groups is calculated.\n",
        "\n",
        "Because hierarchical clustering can be computationally expensive on a large dataset, this section uses a reproducible sample of up to 1,500 customers. This sample is for hierarchical clustering only."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 13,
      "id": "bb6197a5",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "bb6197a5",
        "outputId": "3042c968-1cba-48d6-d25a-1c1f089676d9"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Customers used for hierarchical clustering: 1500\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "sample_n = min(1500, len(X_scaled))\n",
        "rng = np.random.default_rng(RANDOM_STATE)\n",
        "sample_idx = rng.choice(len(X_scaled), size=sample_n, replace=False)\n",
        "X_hier = X_scaled[sample_idx]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Customers used for hierarchical clustering:\", sample_n)"
      ]
    },
