      "source": [
        "## Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "In this case study, K-Means, Hierarchical Clustering, and DBSCAN were implemented on the EastWestAirlines customer dataset containing 3,999 records and 11 features used for clustering after excluding the customer ID.\n",
        "\n",
        "K-Means produced five clusters with a silhouette score of 0.3137. Hierarchical clustering using average linkage produced five clusters on a sample of 1,500 customers and achieved a silhouette score of 0.615. DBSCAN, using `eps = 1.5` and `min_samples = 5`, identified four clusters and classified 185 customers (4.63%) as noise, with a silhouette score of 0.261 when noise points were excluded.\n",
        "\n",
        "The results demonstrate that each algorithm identifies customer groups differently. K-Means is useful for creating a fixed number of segments, hierarchical clustering allows comparison of linkage methods, and DBSCAN identifies dense groups and unusual observations. These methods can help airlines understand customer reward balances, flight activity, and loyalty behaviour.\n",
        "\n"
      ]
